          <compound>
            <id type="integer">742</id>
            <name>trans-Cinnamic acid</name>
            <hmdb-id>HMDB0000930</hmdb-id>
            <description>trans-Cinnamic acid, also known as (e)-cinnamate or (e)-cinnamic acid, belongs to the class of organic compounds known as cinnamic acids. These are organic aromatic compounds containing a benzene and a carboxylic acid group forming 3-phenylprop-2-enoic acid. trans-Cinnamic acid exists as a solid and is considered to be practically insoluble (in water) and relatively neutral. trans-Cinnamic acid has been primarily detected in saliva. Within the cell, trans-cinnamic acid is primarily located in the membrane (predicted from logP). trans-Cinnamic acid exists in all living organisms, ranging from bacteria to humans. trans-Cinnamic acid participates in a number of enzymatic reactions. In particular, trans-Cinnamic acid can be converted into cis-3-(3-carboxyethenyl)-3,5-cyclohexadiene-1,2-diol through the action of the enzyme 3-phenylpropionate dioxygenase system. In addition, trans-Cinnamic acid can be converted into cis-3-(3-carboxyethyl)-3,5-cyclohexadiene-1,2-diol; which is catalyzed by the enzyme 3-phenylpropionate dioxygenase system. trans-Cinnamic acid is a sweet, balsam, and honey tasting compound that can be found in a number of food items such as lupine, peanut, common grape, and grapefruit/pummelo hybrid. This makes trans-cinnamic acid a potential biomarker for the consumption of these food products. trans-Cinnamic acid is a potentially toxic compound. Cinnamic acid has the formula C6H5CHCHCOOH and is an odorless white crystalline acid, which is slightly soluble in water. It has a melting point of 133 degree centigrade and a boiling point of 300 degree centigrade.</description>
